'''''Lysimachia tenella''''', [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] '''''Anagallis tenella''''',<ref name=POWO_701384-1/> known in Britain as the '''bog pimpernel''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=17 October 2014 }}</ref> is a low growing perennial plant found in a variety of damp habitats from calcareous [[sand dune|dune]] slacks to [[bog]]gy and [[peat]]y  [[heath]]s in western and southern Europe and Northwest Africa.<ref name=POWO_701384-1/> In the United Kingdom it is mostly restricted to the western half of the country, although it was more common in the east before land drainage and intensification of farming in that area.<ref>{{cite book |editor-last1=Preston |editor-first1=C.D. |editor-last2=Pearman |editor-first2=D.A. |editor-last3=Dines |editor-first3=T.D. | title=New Atlas of the British and Irish Flora: An Atlas of the Vascular Plants of Britain, Ireland, The Isle of Man and the Channel Islands | year=2002 | publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |isbn=0198510675}}</ref>

The species was first described by [[Carl Linnaeus]] as ''Lysimachia tenella'' in 1753. It was later transferred to the genus ''Anagallis'', but returned to ''Lysimachia'' when ''Anagallis'' was synonymized with that genus,<ref name=POWO_701384-1/> following [[Molecular phylogenetics|molecular phylogenetic]] studies.<ref>{{cite journal|first1=Ulrika |last1=Manns |first2=Arne A. |last2=Anderberg |year = 2005|title = Molecular Phylogeny of ''Anagallis'' (Primulaceae) Based on ITS, ''trn''L-F, and ''ndh''F Sequence Data|journal =   International Journal of Plant Sciences|volume = 166 |pages= 1019–1028|doi =10.1086/449318|issue = 6 |s2cid=83727080 }}</ref>

The Latin [[binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]] ''tenella''  means "tender" or "delicate".<ref name=RHSLG>{{cite book | last=Harrison |first=Lorraine | title=RHS Latin for Gardeners | year=2012 |publisher=Mitchell Beazley | location=United Kingdom | isbn=184533731X }}</ref>

In England this plant is a component of the [[Purple moor grass and rush pastures]] BAP habitat.{{citation needed|date=March 2025}}

